{
  "id": "proj-steel-coat-rack",
  "slug": "steel-coat-rack",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Steel Coat Rack",
  "summary": "A freestanding rack from square tube with a weighted base and forged hooks.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 5,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut the square tube to a 66 in post with the angle grinder and a cut-off wheel. Deburr both ends and wipe the post, plate and rod down with acetone to strip the mill oil.",
    "Cut the round rod into six 10 in lengths. Heat the end of one in the MAP torch flame until it glows orange, then scroll a small curl over the anvil edge with the cross-peen hammer.",
    "Reheat the middle of the rod and bend it around a 1 1/2 in pipe or the anvil horn into a J. Clamp the straight end in the vise to finish the bend, and let every hook air-cool; do not quench mild steel.",
    "Mark the centre of the plate, stand the post on it with the magnetic square, and tack it on two opposite sides. Check it is plumb on both faces, then weld all the way round. Keep the fire extinguisher within reach for every weld.",
    "Weld the hooks to the post, two per face on three faces and staggered between 60 in and 64 in so coats do not pile on each other. Grind the welds smooth with the flap disc.",
    "Tap the plastic end cap into the top of the post. Wipe with acetone again, spray one coat of primer and two coats of hammered black, and stick the rubber pads under the plate corners. The 3/8 in plate is about 15 lb, which keeps a loaded rack from tipping."
